1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,471
Is the shop up? Your server says yes. Tom cat is running, the process is alive.

2
00:00:06,021 --> 00:00:08,283
But a customer adds a mug to the cart,

3
00:00:08,283 --> 00:00:09,355
and gets an error.

4
00:00:09,355 --> 00:00:11,439
Running is not the same as working.

5
00:00:11,989 --> 00:00:14,299
An API test asks the real questions,

6
00:00:14,299 --> 00:00:15,838
the way a program would.

7
00:00:15,838 --> 00:00:16,608
Is it alive?

8
00:00:16,608 --> 00:00:17,891
Is the answer right?

9
00:00:17,891 --> 00:00:19,110
And does it say no,

10
00:00:19,110 --> 00:00:20,200
in the right way,

11
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:21,163
when it should?

12
00:00:21,713 --> 00:00:24,357
Today you test the real Cloud Cart shop,

13
00:00:24,357 --> 00:00:25,613
running on Tom cat,

14
00:00:25,613 --> 00:00:26,539
with pie test.

15
00:00:26,539 --> 00:00:27,002
Health,

16
00:00:27,002 --> 00:00:27,332
cart,

17
00:00:27,332 --> 00:00:28,390
and error codes.

18
00:00:29,940 --> 00:00:31,369
Here is what to expect.

19
00:00:31,919 --> 00:00:35,055
One: what an API test is, and what a health check is.

20
00:00:35,605 --> 00:00:39,808
Two: the answers you check. Status codes, and the data in the reply.

21
00:00:40,358 --> 00:00:45,158
Three: how to write a good API test. Independent, readable, and able to fail.

22
00:00:45,708 --> 00:00:48,169
Then a live demo against the real shop.

23
00:00:48,169 --> 00:00:49,242
We start Tom cat,

24
00:00:49,242 --> 00:00:51,072
write the tests step by step,

25
00:00:51,072 --> 00:00:52,650
and even stop the server,

26
00:00:52,650 --> 00:00:54,732
to see what an outage looks like.

27
00:00:55,282 --> 00:00:59,271
And a lab with six checks, including one that tries to fool your tests.

28
00:01:01,321 --> 00:01:02,686
What is an API test?

29
00:01:03,236 --> 00:01:07,034
An API is the door a program uses to talk to the shop.

30
00:01:07,034 --> 00:01:08,933
Instead of clicking a page,

31
00:01:08,933 --> 00:01:10,269
you send a request,

32
00:01:10,269 --> 00:01:11,605
and read the reply.

33
00:01:11,605 --> 00:01:12,379
No browser,

34
00:01:12,379 --> 00:01:13,082
no screen.

35
00:01:13,082 --> 00:01:14,066
So it is fast,

36
00:01:14,066 --> 00:01:15,332
and very reliable.

37
00:01:15,882 --> 00:01:18,435
It sits in the middle of the test pyramid.

38
00:01:18,435 --> 00:01:19,894
Higher than a unit test,

39
00:01:19,894 --> 00:01:22,265
because the whole running shop answers.

40
00:01:22,265 --> 00:01:23,845
Lower than a browser test,

41
00:01:23,845 --> 00:01:25,973
because there is no page to render.

42
00:01:26,523 --> 00:01:29,060
A health check is the smallest API test.

43
00:01:29,060 --> 00:01:31,089
One request to a health address.

44
00:01:31,089 --> 00:01:33,943
The reply says whether the application is up,

45
00:01:33,943 --> 00:01:35,782
and which version is running.

46
00:01:36,332 --> 00:01:38,310
Operators use it all the time.

47
00:01:38,310 --> 00:01:41,079
A load balancer asks it every few seconds,

48
00:01:41,079 --> 00:01:45,100
and stops sending customers to a server that does not answer.

49
00:01:45,100 --> 00:01:47,342
A pipeline asks it after a deploy,

50
00:01:47,342 --> 00:01:49,452
to decide if the release worked.

51
00:01:50,002 --> 00:01:51,892
Three questions to remember.

52
00:01:51,892 --> 00:01:52,703
Is it alive?

53
00:01:52,703 --> 00:01:53,513
Is it ready?

54
00:01:53,513 --> 00:01:54,458
Is it correct?

55
00:01:54,458 --> 00:01:56,417
Health answers the first two.

56
00:01:56,417 --> 00:01:58,578
Your API tests answer the third.

57
00:02:00,128 --> 00:02:04,203
What do you check in a reply? Two things. The status code, and the data.

58
00:02:04,753 --> 00:02:07,513
The status code is a number from the server.

59
00:02:07,513 --> 00:02:08,956
Two hundred means okay.

60
00:02:08,956 --> 00:02:11,528
Four hundred means the request was wrong.

61
00:02:11,528 --> 00:02:14,289
Five hundred means the server itself failed.

62
00:02:14,839 --> 00:02:16,025
Learn four of them.

63
00:02:16,025 --> 00:02:17,899
Four oh one: you must sign in.

64
00:02:17,899 --> 00:02:20,834
Four oh four: there is nothing at that address.

65
00:02:20,834 --> 00:02:22,396
Four oh nine: a conflict,

66
00:02:22,396 --> 00:02:23,145
for example,

67
00:02:23,145 --> 00:02:24,706
the item is out of stock.

68
00:02:24,706 --> 00:02:27,767
And four hundred: the data you sent is not valid.

69
00:02:28,317 --> 00:02:29,242
Then the data.

70
00:02:29,242 --> 00:02:30,630
The reply is jay son,

71
00:02:30,630 --> 00:02:32,348
a set of names and values.

72
00:02:32,348 --> 00:02:34,859
Check the fields that matter: a price,

73
00:02:34,859 --> 00:02:35,387
a total,

74
00:02:35,387 --> 00:02:35,916
a count.

75
00:02:35,916 --> 00:02:36,907
Not everything,

76
00:02:36,907 --> 00:02:39,154
just what a customer would notice.

77
00:02:39,704 --> 00:02:43,451
Testing the error paths matters as much as the happy path.

78
00:02:43,451 --> 00:02:46,229
A shop that sells to a signed out customer,

79
00:02:46,229 --> 00:02:48,232
or sells an item with no stock,

80
00:02:48,232 --> 00:02:48,813
is a bug.

81
00:02:49,363 --> 00:02:53,011
A good API test checks the status code first, then the data.

82
00:02:54,561 --> 00:02:56,118
Now, how to write a good one.

83
00:02:56,668 --> 00:02:58,771
One: keep every test independent.

84
00:02:58,771 --> 00:03:00,746
Each test makes its own client,

85
00:03:00,746 --> 00:03:03,167
with its own session and its own cart.

86
00:03:03,167 --> 00:03:06,034
No test depends on another one running first.

87
00:03:06,584 --> 00:03:08,178
Two: assert real values.

88
00:03:08,178 --> 00:03:11,833
If the test only checks that the status is two hundred,

89
00:03:11,833 --> 00:03:14,889
a broken shop that always says okay will pass.

90
00:03:14,889 --> 00:03:15,953
Check the total.

91
00:03:15,953 --> 00:03:17,016
Check the count.

92
00:03:17,566 --> 00:03:19,934
Three: name the test after the behavior.

93
00:03:19,934 --> 00:03:21,356
Two keyboards ship free.

94
00:03:21,356 --> 00:03:22,955
That reads like a sentence,

95
00:03:22,955 --> 00:03:24,435
and tells you what broke.

96
00:03:24,985 --> 00:03:27,388
Four: wait for the server to be ready.

97
00:03:27,388 --> 00:03:28,905
After you start Tom cat,

98
00:03:28,905 --> 00:03:30,738
the shop needs a few seconds.

99
00:03:30,738 --> 00:03:32,193
Ask the health address,

100
00:03:32,193 --> 00:03:32,825
in a loop,

101
00:03:32,825 --> 00:03:33,900
until it answers.

102
00:03:33,900 --> 00:03:35,417
Never use a fixed sleep.

103
00:03:35,967 --> 00:03:38,933
Five: a test that can never fail is worth nothing.

104
00:03:38,933 --> 00:03:41,068
Your lab check will try to fool you,

105
00:03:41,068 --> 00:03:42,076
with a fake shop.

106
00:03:42,076 --> 00:03:43,796
We will see that in the task.

107
00:03:45,346 --> 00:03:50,509
Go to the lab folder.

108
00:03:52,059 --> 00:03:53,957
Look around.

109
00:03:54,507 --> 00:03:55,606
A client dot pie,

110
00:03:55,606 --> 00:03:56,575
a tests folder,

111
00:03:56,575 --> 00:03:57,479
the task file,

112
00:03:57,479 --> 00:03:58,836
and a Tom cat folder,

113
00:03:58,836 --> 00:04:01,355
with the shop already placed inside it.

114
00:04:01,905 --> 00:04:08,476
Start the shop.

115
00:04:09,026 --> 00:04:13,271
Tom cat is starting. It needs a few seconds. So never test right away.

116
00:04:13,821 --> 00:04:20,904
The address.

117
00:04:21,454 --> 00:04:28,451
Wait until ready.

118
00:04:30,201 --> 00:04:31,028
There it is.

119
00:04:31,028 --> 00:04:31,785
Status: UP.

120
00:04:31,785 --> 00:04:34,126
Version zero point two point zero.

121
00:04:34,126 --> 00:04:36,260
That reply is the health check.

122
00:04:36,810 --> 00:04:42,890
Ask the API.

123
00:04:44,240 --> 00:04:46,224
Four categories, as jay son.

124
00:04:46,774 --> 00:04:57,142
A status code.

125
00:04:57,692 --> 00:05:00,465
Four oh four. The shop says no, in the right way.

126
00:05:01,015 --> 00:05:04,258
The client.

127
00:05:04,808 --> 00:05:05,660
Twenty lines,

128
00:05:05,660 --> 00:05:07,365
from the standard library.

129
00:05:07,365 --> 00:05:08,349
A client class.

130
00:05:08,349 --> 00:05:09,989
It keeps its own cookies,

131
00:05:09,989 --> 00:05:12,546
so every client is a separate customer.

132
00:05:12,546 --> 00:05:13,792
It sends a request,

133
00:05:13,792 --> 00:05:15,300
and returns the status,

134
00:05:15,300 --> 00:05:16,350
and the jay son.

135
00:05:16,350 --> 00:05:18,186
If the shop does not answer,

136
00:05:18,186 --> 00:05:19,891
it says so in plain words.

137
00:05:20,441 --> 00:05:24,387
A new folder.

138
00:05:24,937 --> 00:05:31,657
Test one.

139
00:05:41,607 --> 00:05:42,165
A client,

140
00:05:42,165 --> 00:05:43,405
a request to health,

141
00:05:43,405 --> 00:05:44,582
and two assertions.

142
00:05:44,582 --> 00:05:46,194
The status is two hundred,

143
00:05:46,194 --> 00:05:47,495
and the body says UP.

144
00:05:48,045 --> 00:05:50,776
Run it.

145
00:05:51,326 --> 00:05:56,937
One passed. Simple, and real. Every test from now on talks to the real running shop.

146
00:05:57,487 --> 00:06:02,479
Test two.

147
00:06:10,429 --> 00:06:12,711
One request, and one check on the count.

148
00:06:13,261 --> 00:06:16,333
Run again.

149
00:06:16,883 --> 00:06:17,779
Two passed.

150
00:06:18,329 --> 00:06:23,407
Test three: the cart.

151
00:06:36,557 --> 00:06:37,343
A new client,

152
00:06:37,343 --> 00:06:39,583
so a new customer with an empty cart.

153
00:06:39,583 --> 00:06:40,793
We post to the cart,

154
00:06:40,793 --> 00:06:41,822
with the product,

155
00:06:41,822 --> 00:06:43,154
and a quantity of two.

156
00:06:43,154 --> 00:06:47,270
Then we assert the subtotal: two times two hundred nineteen dollars,

157
00:06:47,270 --> 00:06:47,814
in cents,

158
00:06:47,814 --> 00:06:49,933
forty-three thousand eight hundred.

159
00:06:50,483 --> 00:06:53,555
Run again.

160
00:06:54,105 --> 00:06:56,217
Three passed. The cart does the math.

161
00:06:56,767 --> 00:07:05,300
Test four: many cases.

162
00:07:15,250 --> 00:07:16,330
One test function,

163
00:07:16,330 --> 00:07:19,029
two cases: a missing path gives four oh four,

164
00:07:19,029 --> 00:07:21,309
and the health path gives two hundred.

165
00:07:21,859 --> 00:07:24,931
Run again.

166
00:07:25,481 --> 00:07:29,107
Four tests became five. Parametrize counted each case.

167
00:07:29,657 --> 00:07:36,953
Now an outage.

168
00:07:37,503 --> 00:07:41,770
Tom cat is stopping. The shop is gone, and our tests do not know that yet.

169
00:07:42,320 --> 00:07:47,248
Run the tests.

170
00:07:48,598 --> 00:07:49,739
Every test fails.

171
00:07:49,739 --> 00:07:52,894
And look at the message: shop is not answering.

172
00:07:52,894 --> 00:07:54,371
Not a confusing crash.

173
00:07:54,371 --> 00:07:55,378
A clear reason.

174
00:07:55,378 --> 00:07:58,667
That is why the client reports it in plain words.

175
00:07:59,217 --> 00:08:04,807
Start it again.

176
00:08:05,357 --> 00:08:07,405
Wait, then test.

177
00:08:10,955 --> 00:08:11,829
Up again.

178
00:08:12,379 --> 00:08:16,411
All green.

179
00:08:16,961 --> 00:08:20,631
Five passed. Same tests, same shop, the outage is over.

180
00:08:21,181 --> 00:08:27,986
Where am I?

181
00:08:28,536 --> 00:08:29,518
One out of six.

182
00:08:29,518 --> 00:08:30,828
The shop is running,

183
00:08:30,828 --> 00:08:32,268
and that check passes.

184
00:08:32,268 --> 00:08:35,215
The test files for the task do not exist yet,

185
00:08:35,215 --> 00:08:36,066
so five fail.

186
00:08:36,066 --> 00:08:36,525
Honest,

187
00:08:36,525 --> 00:08:37,245
and useful.

188
00:08:37,245 --> 00:08:39,864
That is the starting line for your task.

189
00:08:40,414 --> 00:08:42,377
Clean up.

190
00:08:42,927 --> 00:08:45,295
Tom cat stopped, and everything removed.

191
00:08:46,845 --> 00:08:51,005
Your lab task: test the Cloud Cart API yourself. Six checks.

192
00:08:51,555 --> 00:08:54,584
One: start Tom cat, and wait for health to say UP.

193
00:08:55,134 --> 00:08:56,312
Two: health tests,

194
00:08:56,312 --> 00:08:57,228
three of them.

195
00:08:57,228 --> 00:08:58,603
Three: product tests,

196
00:08:58,603 --> 00:08:58,995
three.

197
00:08:58,995 --> 00:09:00,108
Four: cart tests,

198
00:09:00,108 --> 00:09:00,500
three.

199
00:09:00,500 --> 00:09:03,249
The cart must ship free for two keyboards,

200
00:09:03,249 --> 00:09:05,801
and charge shipping for one cheap item.

201
00:09:06,351 --> 00:09:07,499
Five: error tests,

202
00:09:07,499 --> 00:09:08,327
four of them.

203
00:09:08,327 --> 00:09:09,156
Four oh four,

204
00:09:09,156 --> 00:09:09,985
four oh nine,

205
00:09:09,985 --> 00:09:10,750
four oh one,

206
00:09:10,750 --> 00:09:11,834
and four hundred.

207
00:09:12,384 --> 00:09:13,953
Six: the fake shop check.

208
00:09:13,953 --> 00:09:19,289
Lab check runs your tests against a fake shop that answers two hundred to everything.

209
00:09:19,289 --> 00:09:22,113
At least eight of your tests must fail there.

210
00:09:22,113 --> 00:09:23,055
Then lab check,

211
00:09:23,055 --> 00:09:24,373
until six out of six.

212
00:09:26,423 --> 00:09:29,218
Where to find the lab. Pause here, and write it down.

213
00:09:29,768 --> 00:09:31,232
One: the repository,

214
00:09:31,232 --> 00:09:35,552
git hub dot com slash it dash waves slash dev ops dash A I,

215
00:09:35,552 --> 00:09:37,896
in the folder academy slash lab.

216
00:09:38,446 --> 00:09:44,632
Two: the files, lab one thirty-nine dot S H, and shortcuts one thirty-nine dot S H.

217
00:09:45,182 --> 00:09:50,644
Three: install it, with sudo install, into slash user slash local slash S bin.

218
00:09:51,194 --> 00:09:53,044
Four: load the short names,

219
00:09:53,044 --> 00:09:56,197
with source shortcuts one thirty-nine dot S H.

220
00:09:56,197 --> 00:09:57,020
Then lab up,

221
00:09:57,020 --> 00:09:57,705
lab check,

222
00:09:57,705 --> 00:09:58,596
and lab down.

223
00:09:59,146 --> 00:10:00,586
Five: you need java,

224
00:10:00,586 --> 00:10:00,946
curl,

225
00:10:00,946 --> 00:10:01,881
python three,

226
00:10:01,881 --> 00:10:02,817
and pie test.

227
00:10:02,817 --> 00:10:06,272
Lab up downloads Tom cat and Cloud Cart for you.

228
00:10:06,822 --> 00:10:08,795
Six: only on your own machine,

229
00:10:08,795 --> 00:10:10,901
with ports eight one three nine,

230
00:10:10,901 --> 00:10:12,809
and eight one four nine free.

231
00:10:12,809 --> 00:10:14,651
Lab down removes everything.

232
00:10:15,201 --> 00:10:16,844
The link is in the description.

233
00:10:18,894 --> 00:10:22,542
Pass or fail rubric. Six points. All six to pass.

234
00:10:23,092 --> 00:10:25,588
One: Tom cat is running, and health says UP.

235
00:10:26,138 --> 00:10:27,759
Two: three health tests pass.

236
00:10:28,309 --> 00:10:30,251
Three: three product tests pass.

237
00:10:30,801 --> 00:10:33,766
Four: three cart tests pass, with real values.

238
00:10:34,316 --> 00:10:36,030
Five: four error tests pass,

239
00:10:36,030 --> 00:10:37,377
covering four oh four,

240
00:10:37,377 --> 00:10:38,173
four oh nine,

241
00:10:38,173 --> 00:10:38,907
four oh one,

242
00:10:38,907 --> 00:10:39,948
and four hundred.

243
00:10:40,498 --> 00:10:43,634
Six: at least eight of your tests fail against the fake shop.

244
00:10:44,184 --> 00:10:45,485
Pass means all six.

245
00:10:47,535 --> 00:10:49,306
Your turn, on your own machine.

246
00:10:49,856 --> 00:10:54,250
One: lab up, and read the task file. Start Tom cat, and wait for health.

247
00:10:54,800 --> 00:10:58,406
Two: write the health and product tests. Run them after each one.

248
00:10:58,956 --> 00:11:02,582
Three: write the cart tests. Use a new client in every test.

249
00:11:03,132 --> 00:11:08,231
Four: write the error tests. Sign in with the demo account, and send a bad zip code.

250
00:11:08,781 --> 00:11:12,258
Five: lab check, until six out of six. Then lab down.

251
00:11:12,808 --> 00:11:14,643
Pause the video, and begin now.

252
00:11:30,193 --> 00:11:31,068
Quick check.

253
00:11:31,618 --> 00:11:33,559
What does a health check tell an operator?

254
00:11:34,109 --> 00:11:37,672
Which status code means out of stock, and which means sign in first?

255
00:11:38,222 --> 00:11:40,440
Why must every test make its own client?

256
00:11:40,990 --> 00:11:43,038
How do you wait for a server to be ready?

257
00:11:43,588 --> 00:11:49,839
Objective covered: M C fifteen, objective four. API and health tests for Cloud Cart.

258
00:11:50,389 --> 00:11:52,458
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

259
